here P_i denotes the i-th largest prime factor and V_i denotes the i-th component of the Poisson-Dirichlet process. It is reasonable to conjecture that O(1) is achievable.\n\n  * On the Random Sampling of Pairs, with Pedestrian examples\n\nRichard Arratia, Stephen DeSalvo\n\nNov 29 2012 math.PR arXiv:1211.6486v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompletely effective error bounds for Stirling Numbers of the first and second kind via Poisson Approximation (https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.3007)\nciteturn0academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.6 years ago; Authors: Richard Arratia, Stephen DeSalvo\nTitle: Completely effective error bounds for Stirling Numbers of the first and second kind via Poisson Approximation\nAuthors: Richard Arratia, Stephen DeSalvo\nDate: Fri Apr 11 05:51:39 2014\n\nWe provide completely effective error estimates for Stirling numbers of the first and second kind, denoted by $s(n,m)$ and $S(n,m)$, respectively. These bounds are useful for values of $m \\geq n - O(\\sqrt{n})$. An application of our Theorem 5 yields, for example, \\[ s(10^{12},\\ 10^{12}-2\\times 10^6)/10^{35664464} \\in [ 1.87669, 1.876982 ], \\] \\[ S(10^{12},\\ 10^{12}-2\\times 10^6)/10^{35664463} \\in [ 1.30121, 1.306975 ]. \\] The bounds are obtained via Chen-Stein Poisson approximation, using an interpretation of Stirling numbers as the number of ways of placing non-attacking rooks on a chess board. As a corollary to Theorem 5, summarized in Proposition 1, we obtain two simple and explicit asymptotic formulas, one for each of $s(n,m)$ and $S(n,m)$, for the parametrization $m = n - t\\, n^a$, $0 \\leq a \\leq \\frac{1}{2}.$ These asymptotic formulas agree with the ones originally observed by Moser and Wyman in the range $0<a<\\frac{1}{2}$, and they connect with a recent asymptotic expansion by Louchard for $\\frac{1}{2}<a < 1$, hence filling the gap at $a = \\frac{1}{2}$. We also provide 